 - Reasoning state goes through magpie's translation as the vendor wrote it when the agent and the upstream speak the same API (#1445, XYenon): a non-streaming Messages request to a stream-only account, web search on the agent's own API, a plugin, failover. The IR carries it sealed (`Part.Sealed`, `SealedBy`; events `KThinkStart`, `KSealed`, `KTextSig` in [`ir.go`](../../internal/gateway/ir.go)), so each thinking block or reasoning item stays apart, whole or streamed, and a seal is written only for its own API (`sealedBy`). Anthropic's `redacted_thinking` goes back raw (to DeepSeek as a thinking block, below), and a thinking block signed with no text keeps its signature (`aBlock` still writes `"thinking": ""`, #1447), on Claude's subscription too. A Responses reasoning item keeps its id, summary and `encrypted_content`, and goes back to a Responses upstream as it came when it has no reasoning text (`sealedReasoning`; text, another vendor's, is still left out for OpenAI, #1411). Gemini's `thoughtSignature` stays on the call it signed and on text; a later call of a signed step goes without one, and only an unsigned step or Claude on Antigravity gets `skip_thought_signature_validator`. Another API's agent gets the call's signature in its id, as on chat (#687), with its length in the mark (`__ts<n>__`), so an id a client shortened carries no signature and the call goes as unsigned (#1536); it gets no one else's seal. The signature itself is kept in magpie's folder, `gemini-signatures/<key>`, named by its hash (`keepSignature` in [`gemini_sigstore.go`](../../internal/gateway/gemini_sigstore.go)), and the id carries only the key (`__tsk16__<key>`), so an id stays under 64 characters however long the step's signature (#2411: LobeHub's PostgreSQL index turned away a 3KB id). A key magpie no longer has, or whose file doesn't hash to it, reads as no signature; one not read for 90 days is removed. Where the folder can't be written, the id carries the signature as before. Carrying reasoning across APIs (an envelope) isn't done.
 - A translated Responses reply gives Codex the reasoning items a Responses upstream sent, as it sent them; any other upstream's reasoning becomes items magpie made: an `rs_` id and a summary, nothing sealed. Codex hands them back on every turn. A request relayed as is to OpenAI's API or Azure OpenAI goes without them (`withoutBareReasoning`) unless `store` is true, because both look the id up among stored items and answer 400 "Item with id 'rs_…' not found" (#1008). Any other Responses upstream gets them, because a DeepSeek model wants its reasoning back (#388). When one answers 400 naming the input (`refusesInput`: `param` is `input`, or the error names an item by id), as a relay in front of OpenAI does with a message of its own, `passthrough` asks once more without them and, once that works, leaves them out for that provider and model from then on (`bareReasoningRefused`, #1044). Codex's compaction offers no tools, so it is relayed even when its turns were translated. The summary request magpie makes offers none either: `codexInputAs` sends `tools: []` and leaves out the `additional_tools` input item, where Codex's Responses Lite (the entry of a ChatGPT account's gpt-6 model, copied from Codex's own) puts every tool; left in, the model may call one in place of the summary (the likely cause of WuSix's codex/gpt-6-astra "the model wrote no summary" on Discord, not confirmed on a real reply), and a reply with no summary says what it held instead (`answeredWith`). When a provider answers its summary request 404, or 400 for an item it didn't find (`itemNotFound`, a relay in front of either), `codexCompact` asks once more with the conversation as text (`plainCompact`), then compacts locally (#866, #1008). So does the ChatGPT backend's 502 "response protection is unavailable" (`protectionRefused`): a long history refused so was answered as text. Codex also compacts remotely, a `compaction_trigger` on `/responses` that wants exactly one compaction item back, on any provider table named "OpenAI", as CC Switch names its relay tables. magpie points those at `/v1` while a magpie model is on, so `handle` makes the summary there too (`codexInput`, `codexCompact`), and magpie's summaries go back to the model as text (#1301). A summary is wanted only whole (`askWhole`, `holdWriter.wantWhole`): its stream is held to its end, not let through at its first words, so a reply cut short (`errEndedShort`), failed halfway (a 429 "Individual quota reached" after some of it) or ended `incomplete` (`endedIncomplete`, `incompleteReply`) fails over to the next candidate like a failure before any of it, and rests the account as that failure would; one that reached its output limit rests nobody. With nobody left, `codexCompact` answers 502 and never hands Codex the words before the cut as the summary (#2274, huangzuomin: Antigravity cutting each summary short). Codex with context_management's experimental mode compacts in the background at every step while the conversation is over its threshold and drops a failed compaction without a word, so a conversation whose summaries keep failing is asked again every few seconds, each paying for the whole history (1169 compactions in 6 hours there). After `compactFailsMost` (3) failed summaries in a row of one conversation (`prompt_cache_key`) on one model, each within `compactHeldFor` (5 minutes) of the last, the next is answered at once with a 400 naming the last failure, which Codex doesn't ask again, and no model is asked until 5 minutes have passed (`compactFails` in [`codex_compact_guard.go`](../../internal/gateway/codex_compact_guard.go)); a summary that comes clears the count, and a sign-in's 401 or 403 isn't counted. `TestCodexCompactCutSummaryFailsOver`, `TestCodexCompactCutSummaryIsNoSummary`, `TestCodexCompactKeepsFailingIsHeld`.
